WEBVTT

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:12.310
After the festival came the mid-term exams.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.030
We decided to put games on hold
to concentrate on studying, but...

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:23.570
...once the tests began, it was over
super fast, and we went back to gaming.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:29.750
Ah, it feels so long since
the last time I relaxed.

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:31.040
I know, right?

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:36.120
I'm very impressed with Emmy though,
that you can take all the tests in Japanese.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:37.960
Where did you learn Japanese?

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:41.080
After I came here, my grandmother taught me.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:43.430
My grandmother is Japanese!

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:46.870
Wait, how long have you been in Japan?

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:48.660
Four years!

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:54.040
I moved to Kyoto when my dad finally
opened his board game shop here.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:55.400
I see.

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:58.860
By the way, where is your shop, Midori?

00:00:58.860 --> 00:00:59.820
Right here.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:03.500
Hmm, I'm not sure where that is.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:06.660
Well, you only moved here recently. It's hard.

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:10.540
Emmy, Kyoto streets are easy to remember.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:14.630
All the streets cross at
right angles, like a Go board.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:18.130
So if you know which way is north,
you can figure out where to go.

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:19.830
A Go board?

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:23.040
You know, the board used in the game of Go.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:28.520
Oh! Yes, I know Go.
I don't know the rules though...

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:32.410
But I think I get it,
now that I know how to interpret this!

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:35.860
Midori's shop is north of here, so...

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:37.480
This way!

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:39.530
That's west!

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:41.450
Huh...

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:42.620
Could it be...

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:44.570
Emmy, are you...

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:46.570
...bad with directions...?

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:22.460
Hello!

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:24.340
Hey! All together, as usual.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:27.300
Wow, what a cute shop!

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:29.370
Hmm? Who's this?

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:32.370
This is our friend Emmy! She is from Germany!

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:34.140
Oh, from Germany...?

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:37.020
Nice to meet you, I'm Emilia.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:42.190
This might be small and underwhelming
for someone from Germany, but please enjoy.

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:46.900
No, this is not small!
This is my first Japanese game shop visit.

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:49.200
Very interesting...

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:53.200
<i>Elfenland</i>!

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:55.370
<i>Elfenland</i>?

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:59.500
I haven't seen this in so long...
I used to play this a lot back in Germany.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:02.450
The box got smaller.

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:06.250
Yes, but the content is still the same,
board and pieces and all.

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:07.720
Do you want to play?

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:10.050
Oh! Is that okay?

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:12.290
Wow!

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:13.890
What a pretty board!

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:17.120
When I saw it for the first time,
I was impressed, too!

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:22.020
This game is about
traveling in a fantasy world

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:24.690
inhabited by trolls and elves.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:28.750
The boot represents your current location.

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:32.870
You move it and recover
the markers in each town.

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:35.910
Wow! I love traveling!

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:39.870
To move, you use these
vehicle tiles and cards.

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:44.000
Tiles and cards each have
the picture of a vehicle on them.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:49.130
At the start of the game, we deal
eight cards and four tiles to each player.

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:55.100
When the round begins, each player
takes turns placing a tile on the road.

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:58.140
This determines which vehicle
can be used to travel on each road.

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:01.230
What's important to note here is the terrain.

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:02.290
The terrain?

00:05:02.290 --> 00:05:08.290
This world has five terrain types:
Grassland, forest, desert, mountain, and river.

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:12.240
Each terrain type supports
different types of vehicles.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:14.660
Boars can only go on grasslands and forests,

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:17.160
unicorns cannot go on grasslands and rivers,

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:21.450
dragons can go anywhere
except rivers, and so on.

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:23.700
What are these ones and twos...?

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:26.410
That is the cost of travel in cards.

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:27.250
Cost?

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:32.370
For example, if there is a boar tile
on the grasslands, its cost is one,

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:34.580
so you pay one card.

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:39.580
Unicorns going on deserts cost two,
so you pay two cards.

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:41.000
I see!

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:43.290
Can you not go anywhere
if you don't have the right cards?

00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:48.580
In that case, you can pay
any three cards to travel.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:50.410
This is called a caravan.

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:55.790
But it's inefficient, so you should
only use that as a last resort!

00:05:55.790 --> 00:06:00.870
When everyone runs out of cards,
or can't or don't want to play the cards,

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:03.250
the round is over.

00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:09.120
Take four tiles and draw until you have
eight cards, then start the next round.

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:13.790
After four rounds, the player with
the most markers picked up wins.

00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:15.750
Those are the rules.

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:17.620
Thank you! I understand!

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:18.790
Sounds like fun!

00:06:18.790 --> 00:06:20.750
Let's start!

00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:22.080
Yeah!

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:37.330
That's the end of round one.

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:41.580
This is fun! I feel like
I'm really traveling in this world!

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:42.410
Yes.

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:44.580
Come on, let's keep going!

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:49.500
All right! Three more rounds to go.
Let's go to as many towns as possible!

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:51.080
Yeah!

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:56.870
Hey, I was going to put my tile there!

00:06:57.950 --> 00:06:59.370
Good move.

00:07:01.450 --> 00:07:02.950
Yes!

00:07:10.790 --> 00:07:12.540
I can't go any further...

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:15.040
I left four on the board.

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:16.450
Me, too...

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:19.240
Midori-chan got all but two!

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:21.160
Awesome, Midori-chan!

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:24.120
I was so close to getting them all...

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:26.580
So Emmy is the only one left.

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:28.540
Yes, here I go!

00:07:28.540 --> 00:07:31.160
One with a troll, two with the elves,

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:33.500
three with the boar, four with the dragon.

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:37.370
One more dragon makes five, and pay
three cards for the final one--here's six!

00:07:37.370 --> 00:07:38.410
Wow...

00:07:38.410 --> 00:07:41.510
Wow, Emmy! Did you actually get them all?!

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:44.660
Yeah, it went well!

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:48.810
You got me, Emmy... I guess you
aren't bad with directions in games.

00:07:54.410 --> 00:07:57.450
Wow, Emmy! You got them all again!

00:07:57.450 --> 00:08:00.750
You're not bad with directions
in games, are you, Emmy?

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:03.240
Despite still getting lost in the neighborhood!

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:11.370
Emmy, you're going to Japan next week, right?

00:08:11.370 --> 00:08:12.500
Yes...

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:14.330
We'll miss you.

00:08:14.330 --> 00:08:17.040
Wherever you are, we're still friends!

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:20.660
Don't forget about us, okay?

00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:22.140
We'll email you every day.

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:24.220
Hans... Johann...

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:30.700
I promise I won't forget. I'll write you, too.

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:32.000
Liar...!

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:33.110
Anna...

00:08:33.110 --> 00:08:34.940
Emmy, you're a liar!

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:38.450
Emmy?!

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:39.740
Wh-What happened?!

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:42.160
You made fun of her sense of directions!

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:44.700
Huh?! Wait, no, that was...

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:49.500
I'm sorry, it's not that...
I just remembered something...

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:54.500
This was the last game
I played with my friends in Germany.

00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:59.590
I couldn't keep my promise
with those friends...

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:02.050
Your promise...?

00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:07.310
Anna... My closest friend.

00:09:07.930 --> 00:09:14.650
We grew up together. We both love games.
We promised each other...

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:18.740
...that one day, we would make a game together.

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:28.620
We were separated,
but our dreams are still the same.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:29.700
Dreams...?

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:37.550
Yes, my dream is to make a game
that can make kids all over the world smile!

00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:40.420
She's trying to make a game, too...?!

00:09:40.420 --> 00:09:44.370
I see! You're trying to
become a game designer, too!

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:45.590
So you and Midori-chan...

00:09:46.790 --> 00:09:47.720
Murder vibes?!

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:50.270
Midori-chan...?

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:52.540
What's wrong?

00:09:52.540 --> 00:09:55.910
Oh... um, uh...

00:09:55.910 --> 00:09:59.750
I just remembered,
I had to do something today! I forgot!

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:03.200
Sorry, see you later...

00:10:04.790 --> 00:10:06.450
Midori-chan...

00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:11.410
Well, now, I guess she's going
to be a fledgling for a while longer.

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:15.460
Midori-chan...

00:10:15.910 --> 00:10:19.370
Midori is trying to be
a game designer, too, right?

00:10:19.370 --> 00:10:20.920
How did you know, Emmy?

00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:22.630
Well, that's...

00:10:22.630 --> 00:10:26.260
...to become a game author myself!

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:31.330
I was so happy to learn someone else
in Japan had the same dream as me.

00:10:31.330 --> 00:10:33.000
I see!

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:36.450
But it doesn't seem like
Midori wants to discuss it...

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:40.070
As two people with the same dream,
I would love to share thoughts on this...

00:10:40.750 --> 00:10:41.910
Emmy...

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:47.620
Hey, I want to discuss something
with the two of you.

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:54.080
{Between}

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:00.950
Emmy wants to make games...?

00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:06.290
She grew up in Germany, the center of gaming
culture... What would she come up with?

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:10.200
I want to play Emmy's game, too!

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:14.500
But... what if it's extremely fun?

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:18.870
What if her talents are much better than mine?

00:11:18.870 --> 00:11:23.620
This silly pride is making me
afraid of seeing her game...

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:25.000
I'm sorry.

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:28.540
You're not a designer.

00:11:28.540 --> 00:11:32.450
Shut up! I know that!

00:11:32.450 --> 00:11:35.040
I know that...

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:38.870
Huh? Stuff we did when we were younger?

00:11:38.870 --> 00:11:43.250
Yes. I want to learn what
traditional games children play in Japan.

00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:46.750
So that's the type of thing
you're interested in.

00:11:46.750 --> 00:11:50.910
In Germany, both board games and playtime
activities for children are called <i>spiel</i> .

00:11:50.910 --> 00:11:53.580
Both fall under "fun things."

00:11:53.580 --> 00:11:57.830
Perhaps it could be a hint for making games!

00:11:57.830 --> 00:12:00.660
Hmm, child's play, huh?

00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:03.040
Like tag or hide-and-seek?

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:05.530
Is there something more Japanese?

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:07.990
How about Daruma-Doll Fell Down?

00:12:08.330 --> 00:12:09.110
Oh!

00:12:09.110 --> 00:12:10.200
Good one!

00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:12.830
How does that go?

00:12:12.830 --> 00:12:14.950
Shall we go try it now?

00:12:14.950 --> 00:12:15.660
Yes!

00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:20.870
So the rules are like...

00:12:20.870 --> 00:12:24.290
Well, I actually haven't played this too much.

00:12:24.290 --> 00:12:25.830
Okay, I'll take over.

00:12:25.830 --> 00:12:27.290
Thank you!

00:12:27.290 --> 00:12:31.790
In Daruma-Doll Fell Down,
one player becomes the Oni.

00:12:31.790 --> 00:12:34.000
Oni? What's an Oni?

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:39.290
It's a Japanese monster!
Big and scary, with horns like this!

00:12:39.290 --> 00:12:40.500
Oh!

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:44.200
We do rock-paper-scissors to decide
who becomes the Oni, then...

00:12:44.200 --> 00:12:47.450
Okay, start at that tree!

00:12:47.450 --> 00:12:51.040
The other children start from this line.

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:58.700
The Oni turns around and chants, "Daruma-doll
fell down," and the children approach.

00:12:58.700 --> 00:13:00.950
Daruma-doll fell down!

00:13:00.950 --> 00:13:05.500
When the Oni finishes saying that
and turns around, the children stop.

00:13:05.500 --> 00:13:08.540
While the Oni is looking,
the children can't move.

00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:12.750
If you move, you get captured by the Oni.

00:13:12.750 --> 00:13:15.790
Oh! What happens when you get captured?

00:13:15.790 --> 00:13:20.160
The captured child holds hands
with the Oni and awaits rescue.

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:26.910
If another child tags the Oni while
the Oni is chanting, the child is rescued.

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:28.000
Daruma-doll...

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:28.870
Tag!

00:13:28.870 --> 00:13:30.700
One, two, three, four, five...

00:13:30.700 --> 00:13:33.080
The Oni then counts to ten,
and during that time,

00:13:33.080 --> 00:13:38.330
all of the children try to get as
far away from the Oni as possible.

00:13:38.330 --> 00:13:39.410
Stop!

00:13:39.410 --> 00:13:42.410
When the Oni says "stop," everyone stops.

00:13:42.410 --> 00:13:45.040
I remember now, that sounds right.

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:48.750
So from here, I think the rules
vary from region to region.

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:51.160
The child who successfully tagged the Oni
decides how many steps the Oni takes.

00:13:51.160 --> 00:13:52.120
Steps?

00:13:52.120 --> 00:13:55.750
Like five big steps or ten small steps.

00:13:55.750 --> 00:13:57.160
Five big steps!

00:13:57.160 --> 00:14:01.020
If the Oni can't tag anyone from there,
they play the Oni again.

00:13:59.540 --> 00:14:01.020
One, two, three...

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:08.080
Also, if the Oni can't catch anyone before
they are tagged, they play the Oni again.

00:14:08.080 --> 00:14:10.230
So that's the game...

00:14:10.230 --> 00:14:13.540
<i>Danke</i> (Thanks), Miki! This is very interesting!

00:14:13.540 --> 00:14:17.500
Actually, there is a very
similar game in Europe as well!

00:14:17.500 --> 00:14:17.950
Huh?

00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:19.830
In Germany, it is called "Ox on the Mountain."

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:23.870
In England, I think it's called
"Green Light, Red Light."

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:28.540
Both involve approaching a player
while their back is turned to tag them.

00:14:28.540 --> 00:14:29.830
I see!

00:14:29.830 --> 00:14:32.080
I thought it was a Japanese game...

00:14:32.080 --> 00:14:35.450
But this is different from the European version.

00:14:35.450 --> 00:14:37.830
The Japanese version is more complex.

00:14:37.830 --> 00:14:39.500
Okay, let's play!

00:14:39.500 --> 00:14:40.790
Yeah!

00:14:41.870 --> 00:14:43.250
Here I go!

00:14:43.250 --> 00:14:46.410
Okay, first step!

00:14:47.450 --> 00:14:49.580
Daruma-doll...

00:14:49.580 --> 00:14:51.620
...fell down!

00:14:55.040 --> 00:14:57.450
Daruma-doll...

00:14:57.450 --> 00:14:59.290
...fell down!

00:14:59.290 --> 00:15:00.660
Hey, Midori-chan!

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:02.700
Huh?! Did I move?

00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:04.830
Yes, your left hand.

00:15:04.830 --> 00:15:06.830
Don't worry, Midori-chan!

00:15:06.830 --> 00:15:09.200
We will rescue you!

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:10.700
Here's my hand.

00:15:13.790 --> 00:15:15.790
Here we go!

00:15:15.790 --> 00:15:18.000
Daruma-doll...

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:19.410
...fell...

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:19.410
Tag!

00:15:19.410 --> 00:15:21.790
One, two, three, four, five,
six, seven, eight, nine, ten!

00:15:19.410 --> 00:15:21.790
Run!

00:15:21.790 --> 00:15:23.080
Stop!

00:15:24.290 --> 00:15:26.450
Aya-chan, did you tag me?

00:15:26.450 --> 00:15:27.620
Yes!

00:15:27.620 --> 00:15:29.580
Okay, call the steps!

00:15:29.580 --> 00:15:31.270
Three big steps!

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:34.450
Huh...? Three steps...?

00:15:34.450 --> 00:15:38.070
One... two... three.

00:15:38.910 --> 00:15:40.740
Well, naturally...

00:15:40.870 --> 00:15:43.790
Three steps? Impossible!

00:15:43.790 --> 00:15:46.040
Aya, you cheated!

00:15:46.040 --> 00:15:49.120
But you said I could decide...

00:15:49.500 --> 00:15:54.620
If you think about it, the child getting
to decide the Oni's steps seems odd.

00:15:54.620 --> 00:15:58.260
That part should be fixed
to ten or 15 steps or something.

00:15:58.660 --> 00:16:01.500
I think it's played that way in some regions.

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:06.450
How was it, Emmy? Did it help at all?

00:16:06.450 --> 00:16:11.360
Hmm... I have one question.

00:16:11.660 --> 00:16:14.320
How does this game end?

00:16:14.540 --> 00:16:16.160
Huh? End?

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:18.450
I guess it doesn't really have one...

00:16:18.450 --> 00:16:21.030
Like when the school bell rings, I guess?

00:16:21.160 --> 00:16:27.080
Now that I think about it,
a lot of children's games are endless like that.

00:16:27.080 --> 00:16:29.750
Like tag and kick the can.

00:16:29.750 --> 00:16:32.700
I see, endless...

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:34.620
What's wrong, Emmy?

00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:37.660
These rules have some issues.

00:16:37.660 --> 00:16:40.410
Well, it's classic child's play.

00:16:40.410 --> 00:16:43.000
Once you get used to modern games,
it won't be as interesting.

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:45.540
That's not true, Midori.

00:16:45.540 --> 00:16:49.830
Any game has the possibility to be fun.

00:16:49.830 --> 00:16:54.620
We're going to work on making
Daruma-Doll Fell Down more interesting!

00:16:54.620 --> 00:16:56.790
Daruma-Doll Fell Down...

00:16:56.790 --> 00:16:58.330
More interesting...?

00:16:58.330 --> 00:17:03.500
Yes. This game currently
has a big issue, which is...

00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:06.450
...the children have no rewards.

00:17:07.660 --> 00:17:09.370
Rewards?

00:17:09.370 --> 00:17:14.160
In the current rules,
the children try to tag the Oni.

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:17.370
However, tagging the Oni
just starts the next round.

00:17:17.370 --> 00:17:19.620
Go again?

00:17:19.620 --> 00:17:23.160
Just repeating the same thing.
This is not rewarding.

00:17:23.160 --> 00:17:26.370
In addition, when going
to rescue a captured child,

00:17:26.370 --> 00:17:27.160
the child who tags the Oni...

00:17:27.160 --> 00:17:28.040
Tag!

00:17:27.160 --> 00:17:28.040
the child who tags the Oni...

00:17:28.040 --> 00:17:29.540
Stop!

00:17:29.540 --> 00:17:33.080
...is at a disadvantage
relative to the other children.

00:17:33.080 --> 00:17:37.870
In other words, the children face
many risks, but get no reward.

00:17:37.870 --> 00:17:41.000
There is no motivation
for them to tag the Oni.

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:42.450
I see!

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:46.040
I've never even thought of this that way.

00:17:46.040 --> 00:17:50.120
You've got to be kidding me...
All that, after just one play?!

00:17:50.120 --> 00:17:55.040
But if we add an end and a reward,
this would become more interesting!

00:17:55.040 --> 00:17:59.790
First, if a child tags the Oni,
the child gets one point!

00:17:59.790 --> 00:18:02.660
Then, if the Oni can tag
the children who escape

00:18:02.660 --> 00:18:06.200
within a set number of steps,
the Oni gets one point!

00:18:06.200 --> 00:18:09.540
Also, let's take turns playing as the Oni!

00:18:09.540 --> 00:18:15.830
After everyone plays the Oni once each,
the game is over. Highest points wins.

00:18:15.830 --> 00:18:18.370
Are you sure that's enough to make this fun?

00:18:18.370 --> 00:18:20.580
Not much has changed...

00:18:20.580 --> 00:18:24.500
Yes, I think so! Now we must test it out.

00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:27.000
Here I go!

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:28.790
Okay!

00:18:28.790 --> 00:18:29.750
Daruma-doll...

00:18:29.750 --> 00:18:31.120
Tag her...

00:18:29.750 --> 00:18:31.120
Daruma-doll...

00:18:31.120 --> 00:18:32.450
Get one point...

00:18:31.120 --> 00:18:32.450
Daruma-doll...

00:18:32.450 --> 00:18:35.660
Huh? It feels different from before...

00:18:32.450 --> 00:18:35.660
...fell...

00:18:35.660 --> 00:18:36.750
...down!

00:18:40.830 --> 00:18:44.700
Daruma-doll fell down!

00:18:46.250 --> 00:18:47.250
Aya!

00:18:47.250 --> 00:18:49.080
Oh, no!

00:18:49.080 --> 00:18:55.000
Tagging to get one point--
meaning the children have to compete!

00:18:55.000 --> 00:19:01.290
As a result, the children want to move faster,
which makes it harder to stop!

00:19:01.290 --> 00:19:03.580
Daruma-doll...

00:19:03.580 --> 00:19:06.370
...fell down!

00:19:06.370 --> 00:19:10.200
Very good, you two!

00:19:10.200 --> 00:19:12.160
Impressive, Emmy...

00:19:12.160 --> 00:19:16.450
Through a few simple changes,
she added a powerful trade-off mechanic...

00:19:16.450 --> 00:19:20.290
Daruma-Doll Fell Down
became a real game!

00:19:20.290 --> 00:19:23.080
Daruma-doll fell...

00:19:23.080 --> 00:19:24.000
Tag!

00:19:23.080 --> 00:19:24.000
Daruma-doll fell...

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:26.660
One, two, three, four, five,
six, seven, eight, nine, ten!

00:19:26.660 --> 00:19:28.250
Stop!

00:19:28.250 --> 00:19:34.370
You're all very fast... We should
decide how many steps the Oni can take.

00:19:34.370 --> 00:19:40.200
One, two, three, four, five,
six, seven, eight, nine...

00:19:40.200 --> 00:19:41.120
...ten!

00:19:41.120 --> 00:19:44.870
Mm, so close! Too bad...

00:19:44.870 --> 00:19:47.120
So Miki gets one point!

00:19:47.120 --> 00:19:47.790
Yes.

00:19:47.790 --> 00:19:49.450
I'm jealous, Miki-chan!

00:19:49.450 --> 00:19:51.780
You're right, this is more motivating!

00:19:52.080 --> 00:19:55.080
Okay, let's keep going!

00:19:55.080 --> 00:19:59.410
Here we go! One, two, three, four,

00:19:59.410 --> 00:20:04.170
five, six, seven, eight, nine, ten! Tag!

00:20:04.620 --> 00:20:06.830
Yay! Three points total!

00:20:06.830 --> 00:20:09.250
You win, Aya! Congratulations!

00:20:09.250 --> 00:20:12.870
I didn't think this could be so fun!

00:20:12.870 --> 00:20:15.470
I know! It was just a small change, but...

00:20:15.830 --> 00:20:20.560
Alex Randolph said
all games are works in progress.

00:20:21.790 --> 00:20:26.190
Alex Randolph... That godlike figure
in the world of German-style games...

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:30.860
Soccer and rugby were
born from ancient football.

00:20:30.860 --> 00:20:34.990
<i>Chaturanga</i> began in India,
then transformed into chess and Shogi.

00:20:36.120 --> 00:20:40.420
New games emerge through
the constant evolution of games.

00:20:40.660 --> 00:20:42.880
Constant evolution...

00:20:43.580 --> 00:20:50.040
We, as aspiring game designers,
need to understand this, right, Midori?

00:20:50.040 --> 00:20:53.500
Yes, you're right... Wait, what?!

00:20:53.500 --> 00:20:55.080
Huh? What...? Why...?

00:20:55.080 --> 00:20:57.540
Did you tell her?!

00:20:57.540 --> 00:20:59.790
No, she knew all along!

00:20:59.790 --> 00:21:00.620
Huh?

00:21:00.620 --> 00:21:05.980
She wanted to talk about that stuff
with you, but never had the chance to.

00:21:06.250 --> 00:21:08.750
That's when Miki talked to me.

00:21:08.750 --> 00:21:09.950
Huh?

00:21:09.950 --> 00:21:14.200
Okay, Miki! In that case, leave it to Emmy!

00:21:14.200 --> 00:21:16.790
Concerns about games
shall be solved using games!

00:21:16.790 --> 00:21:17.620
Huh?

00:21:17.620 --> 00:21:20.000
With games? What do you mean?

00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:22.330
You'll find out tomorrow!

00:21:23.660 --> 00:21:26.700
I was wondering what she was going to do,

00:21:26.700 --> 00:21:31.470
but I totally forgot because this was so fun!

00:21:32.500 --> 00:21:37.810
So all of this today was because
I couldn't come out to Emmy...?

00:21:33.160 --> 00:21:35.040
You were impressive, Aya!

00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:41.870
You guys are so nosy...

00:21:41.870 --> 00:21:44.330
Was it presumptuous of us...?

00:21:44.330 --> 00:21:45.310
Thank you.

00:21:47.610 --> 00:21:50.570
Emmy, I'm sorry I didn't tell you.

00:21:50.570 --> 00:21:54.280
My dream is to become a game designer, too.

00:21:55.330 --> 00:21:58.990
Can you try my game out sometime?

00:22:00.790 --> 00:22:04.500
Of course! I can't wait
to play your game, Midori!

00:23:37.750 --> 00:23:40.430
I hate you, Dad!

00:23:40.430 --> 00:23:43.640
How can you break your promise
to go to the Christmas party with me?!

00:23:44.120 --> 00:23:46.430
I won't forgive you,
even if you play games with me!
